      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Gabions — wire baskets filled with stone — are among the oldest engineered structures in civil works and among the most commonly mis-specified products in the wire mesh trade. The failure mode is almost always the same: a buyer compares two quotes, picks the cheaper, and discovers eighteen months later that "gabion" covered a 2.0 mm wire basket with 30 g/m² of zinc in an application designed for 2.7 mm GALFAN wire. This guide gives you the specification framework, realistic price bands, and the installation rules that decide whether a gabion wall lasts 5 years or 50. Product details live on our <a href="/products/gabion-baskets/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">gabion baskets page</a>; application context is in <a href="/applications/construction/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">construction applications</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Anatomy -->
    <section id="anatomy" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Gabion Anatomy: What You Are Actually Buying</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        A gabion basket is a box woven from hexagonal double-twist wire mesh — the same technology as chicken wire, but at civil-engineering scale. "Double-twist" means each intersection is formed by two wires twisting around each other; if a wire breaks, the double twist holds the mesh together instead of unravelling like single-twist hex mesh. Panels are factory-woven to the basket dimensions, folded flat for shipping, and assembled on site by lacing the faces together with binding wire or helical (corkscrew) connectors.
      </p>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Standard basket sizes follow a modular logic: 1 × 1 × 0.5 m, 1 × 1 × 1 m, 2 × 1 × 0.5 m, 2 × 1 × 1 m, 3 × 1 × 0.5 m, 3 × 1 × 1 m, and 4 × 1 × 1 m, with 0.5 m and 1 m heights dominating. Custom sizes are routine from cluster factories but drive price up and container utilization down — sticking to standard modules is usually worth it. A 2 × 1 × 1 m basket holds 2 m³ of stone; a wall of stacked 1 m³ baskets is the universal mental model for quantity estimation.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Specs -->
    <section id="specs" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Wire Diameter, Mesh Size, and Standards</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Three mesh opening sizes cover nearly all civil applications, each pairing with defined wire diameters. The governing reference standards are ASTM A975/A975M (metallic-coated steel gabion baskets and revet mattresses) and EN 10223-3 (hexagonal mesh gabions); specify one by number in your purchase order — it forces the factory onto defined wire, coating, and mesh-tolerance territory.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-theme-card border-b border-theme">
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Mesh Opening (nominal)</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Body Wire Diameter</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Selvedge Wire Diameter</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Typical Use</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-theme-secondary">
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">60 × 80 mm</td>
              <td class="p-3">2.2–2.4 mm (2.7 mm for aggressive environments)</td>
              <td class="p-3">3.0–3.4 mm</td>
              <td class="p-3">Standard retaining walls, channel lining, architectural</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">80 × 100 mm</td>
              <td class="p-3">2.4–2.7 mm (3.0 mm for heavy duty)</td>
              <td class="p-3">3.4–3.8 mm</td>
              <td class="p-3">River training, scour protection, weirs</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">100 × 120 mm</td>
              <td class="p-3">2.7–3.0 mm</td>
              <td class="p-3">3.8–4.0 mm</td>
              <td class="p-3">Large rock, erosion blankets, cost-driven mass fill</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Two specification rules prevent 90% of disputes. First, <strong>always specify selvedge wire separately</strong>: the perimeter edges carry the lacing loads and filling impact; a basket with 2.7 mm body wire and 2.7 mm edges is a different (weaker) product than 2.7/3.4 mm. Second, state <strong>tolerances in the PO</strong>: mesh opening tolerance per EN 10223-3 (+16%/−4% nominal is the standard frame), wire diameter tolerance (plus 0.08/−0.04 mm is common practice), and minimum zinc/coating masses. A factory that cannot state these numbers on the proforma invoice is quoting a product it has not fully defined.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-theme-secondary bg-opacity-10 border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">The Weight Check</h3>
        <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
          Gabion quotes are weight-driven, and wire diameter is easily shaved. A 2 × 1 × 1 m, 80 × 100 mm basket in 2.7/3.4 mm GALFAN wire weighs roughly 8–10 kg empty. Before paying balances, weigh sampled baskets against the factory's declared theoretical weight — our <a href="/tools/welded-wire-mesh-weight-calculator/" class="text-theme-primary underline">mesh weight calculator approach</a> applies to hexagonal netting too: total wire length per m² × wire weight per meter, then compare to the scale. A 10% shortfall means roughly a millimeter has disappeared somewhere.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Coating -->
    <section id="coating" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">GALFAN vs Galvanized vs PVC Coating</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        The coating decides service life, and service life decides lifecycle cost. There are three systems on the market, and the price ladder between them is smaller than most buyers expect relative to the performance gap:
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-theme-card border-b border-theme">
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">System</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Composition</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Typical Life (Environment-Dependent)</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Relative Price</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-theme-secondary">
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Hot-dip galvanized</td>
              <td class="p-3">Zinc coating, typically 220–275 g/m² (Class A range) on the wire</td>
              <td class="p-3">2–10 years; upper end only in dry, non-aggressive soils</td>
              <td class="p-3">1.0× (baseline)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">GALFAN (Zn-5%Al)</td>
              <td class="p-3">Zinc with ~5% aluminum and rare-earth mischmetal, ≥ 245 g/m² typical</td>
              <td class="p-3">2–3× plain zinc life; 20–30+ years in many soils per EN 10244-2 testing</td>
              <td class="p-3">1.1–1.3×</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">PVC-coated over galvanized</td>
              <td class="p-3">0.4–0.6 mm polymer coat over ≥ 220 g/m² zinc base wire</td>
              <td class="p-3">25–50+ years claimed in mild environments; 15–25 realistic in UV-exposed sites</td>
              <td class="p-3">1.4–1.8×</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Practical selection guidance: in dry inland conditions with neutral soil, plain hot-dip galvanized at 245 g/m² is honest value. In anything wet, saline, acidic, or polluted — riverbanks, coastal works, mine drainage, de-iced roadside — step up to GALFAN; the 10–30% premium routinely triples service life, and ASTM A975 explicitly recognizes Zn-5%Al wire. Specify PVC where the visual matters (architectural walls) or where abrasion is low and UV is manageable, and require the <strong>zinc layer under the PVC to be stated</strong> — coating a thinly-galvanized wire in plastic is a known corner-cutting move that fails from the inside out. Whatever the system, put the coating mass in g/m² and the standard (ASTM A641 for zinc wire, EN 10244-2 for GALFAN) into the contract, and verify with a coating-thickness gauge at inspection.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Accessories -->
    <section id="accessories" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Diaphragms, Lids, and Accessories</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        A gabion wall's structural behavior depends on details buyers routinely forget to quote:
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-theme-secondary space-y-2 mb-6">
        <li><strong>Internal diaphragms.</strong> Baskets longer than 1 m in any horizontal direction need internal dividing panels spaced at maximum 1 m intervals — a 2 × 1 × 1 m basket has one diaphragm, a 3 × 1 × 1 m has two. Diaphragms prevent the fill from bulging the long faces outward under lateral earth pressure. Quotes that omit diaphragms are selling you a balloon.</li>
        <li><strong>Lids.</strong> Every basket needs a lid panel laced down after filling. Confirm lids are included per basket, same mesh and coating as the body, and count them in the quote.</li>
        <li><strong>Lacing (binding) wire.</strong> Budget 3% of the basket weight in lacing wire for hand-tying, or order helical connectors as a faster mechanical alternative. Either way, lacing wire must match the basket coating system — mixing plain zinc lacing into a GALFAN basket creates a galvanic couple at every joint.</li>
        <li><strong>Bracing (stiffener) wire.</strong> Internal tie wires that hold opposite faces apart during filling — specify them with the order (usually supplied as offcuts by the installer, but confirming diameter and spacing avoids improvisation).</li>
        <li><strong>Gabion mattresses (revet mattresses).</strong> For channel lining and scour protection, the same product at 0.17–0.3 m thickness with finer 60 × 80 mm mesh; specify them separately rather than assuming basket quotes cover mattress geometry.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        When comparing quotes line by line, a legitimately cheaper quote sometimes just has fewer diaphragms or thinner lids. Normalize on: baskets with stated wire diameters, diaphragm count, lids included, and lacing wire allowance — then compare per cubic meter of installed basket volume.
      </p>
    </section>

