            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">GALFAN, 60×80, 2.7/3.4 mm, ≥ 245 g/m²</td>
              <td class="p-3">$16–24</td>
              <td class="p-3">The standard spec for water-adjacent civil work</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">GALFAN, 80×100, 2.7/3.4 mm, ≥ 245 g/m²</td>
              <td class="p-3">$15–22</td>
              <td class="p-3">Slightly less wire per m² than 60×80; common for river training</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">PVC-coated (over 245 g/m² zinc), 60×80, 2.4/3.0 mm core</td>
              <td class="p-3">$22–32</td>
              <td class="p-3">Architectural and long-life specs; check UV stability of the polymer</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Heavy duty, 3.0/4.0 mm GALFAN, any mesh</td>
              <td class="p-3">$26–45</td>
              <td class="p-3">High-energy waterways, military, impact-prone sites</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Freight is the hidden second price. Gabions pack flat and dense: a 20ft container holds roughly 700–900 m³ of basket volume depending on wire spec, at $1,200–2,500 ocean freight to major Western ports. Below about one container's worth, per-unit freight can exceed the basket cost — one reason factories quote MOQs near a full container. Model the landed number with the <a href="/tools/fob-price-calculator/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">FOB price calculator</a> and confirm the load plan with the <a href="/tools/container-loading-calculator/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">container loading calculator</a>; gabions are one product where the loading plan genuinely changes the quote comparison.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-theme-secondary bg-opacity-10 border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">Worked Budget Example</h3>
        <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
          1,000 m of 2 m-high wall in 1 m³ baskets stacked two high: 1,000 m³ of baskets. GALFAN 80×100 at $18/m³ FOB = $18,000 product, plus one 20ft + partial container freight of ~$2,000 landed to port, plus stone fill locally sourced. The same wall in budget 220 g/m² galvanized saves maybe $3,000 and loses 15–20 years of service life in a wet climate. Run that arithmetic for your site before choosing the coating.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Installation -->
    <section id="installation" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Installation Basics and Bracing</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Gabions fail at installation more often than in the factory. The sequence matters as much as the spec:
      </p>

      <ol class="list-decimal list-inside text-theme-secondary space-y-2 mb-6">
        <li><strong>Prepare the foundation.</strong> Level and compacted, with a geotextile separation layer where soil is fine-grained — this stops fines migrating into the stone fill and destabilizing the base.</li>
        <li><strong>Assemble and position empty baskets.</strong> Erect the base, sides, and end panels; lace joints with the specified wire at maximum 100–150 mm spacing, corners doubled. Align the empty row perfectly — straightening a filled basket is not possible.</li>
        <li><strong>Anchor back-to-back units.</strong> Where two baskets face each other across a wall, connect them so they act as one structure.</li>
        <li><strong>Fill in stages with bracing.</strong> This is the step that decides wall geometry. Fill each basket to one-third height, then install <strong>bracing (stiffener) wires</strong> tying the front face to the back face — typically two ties per m² at that level. Repeat at two-thirds height, then fill to 25–50 mm above the top for settlement and install the lid. Unbraced filling is how walls acquire the pregnant bulge that telegraphs failure.</li>
        <li><strong>Stone selection.</strong> Use hard, durable, non-frost-susceptible stone 100–200 mm for 80×100 and 100×120 mesh, 80–150 mm for 60×80. Rounded river stone hand-placed at exposed faces gives the architectural finish; internal fill can be rougher quarry run.</li>
        <li><strong>Lace lids and proceed upward.</strong> Lids down, then stack the next course with offsets (like brickwork — never continuous vertical joints), connecting courses with lacing wire. The top course is usually filled with slightly better stone since it is most visible and most exposed.</li>
        <li><strong>Inspect as you go.</strong> Check face bulge with a straightedge at each bracing stage; 25–50 mm tolerance is normal, beyond that re-brace before the next lift.</li>
      </ol>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Hand labor dominates gabion installation cost — typically 1.5–3 worker-hours per m³ for filling and lacing with crane-assist, more without. Budget that alongside the basket price; a $2/m³ basket saving evaporates if the fill sequence has to be redone.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- FAQ -->
    <section id="faq" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <div class="space-y-4">
        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">What mesh size and wire diameter do I need for a retaining wall?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            For standard gravity retaining walls in non-aggressive soil: 60 × 80 mm or 80 × 100 mm mesh, 2.4–2.7 mm body wire, 3.0–3.4 mm selvedge wire, GALFAN coating. Step up to 2.7/3.4 mm GALFAN minimum for water-adjacent or saline conditions, and 3.0/4.0 mm heavy duty for high-energy waterways. Match stone size to mesh: 100–200 mm stone for 80×100 and 100×120 openings, 80–150 mm for 60×80.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Is GALFAN worth the extra cost over plain galvanized?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            In wet, saline, acidic, or polluted environments, almost always. Zn-5%Al GALFAN wire per EN 10244-2 delivers roughly two to three times the corrosion life of equivalent plain zinc in the same soil or water, for a price premium of only 10–30%. In permanently dry, benign conditions, quality 245 g/m² plain galvanized is honest value. The deciding question is not the purchase price but the cost of rebuilding a failed wall — usually 10–20× the basket cost in labor and machinery.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">How long do gabion baskets last?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Service life tracks the coating and the site: budget galvanized (200 g/m² class) in wet or acidic conditions can show corrosion in 2–5 years; quality 245 g/m² galvanized in dry neutral soil runs 10–20 years; GALFAN typically doubles-to-triples those figures; PVC-coated systems claim 25–50 years where UV exposure is limited. Stone quality matters too — soft or frost-prone rock degrading inside the basket accelerates wire wear.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Do gabion baskets need diaphragms?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Yes, wherever a basket exceeds 1 m in length or width. Diaphragms are internal dividing panels at maximum 1 m spacing that stop the stone fill from bulging the long faces outward under load — a 3 × 1 × 1 m basket has two diaphragms. Confirm the diaphragm count, wire diameter, and coating in every quote; baskets quoted without diaphragms are structurally different products even if outer dimensions match.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">What is the minimum order for gabion baskets from China?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Practically, about one 20ft container of packed basket volume — roughly 700–900 m³ — because below that freight per m³ climbs steeply. Many factories will quote a 100–300 m³ trial order at a per-m³ premium, which is sensible for testing a supplier before a project award. Gabions are a poor LCL product: bulky, awkward, and freight-dominated. Plan quantities around the container with the <a href="/tools/container-loading-calculator/" class="text-theme-primary underline">container loading calculator</a> and compare total landed cost via the <a href="/tools/fob-price-calculator/" class="text-theme-primary underline">FOB price calculator</a>.
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>

